A||||11||Brandon86SE||Changing plugs and wires||||5||
Z||000000||Brandon86SE||11-19-2001||12:24 AM||||I bought new plugs and wires for my baby on the weekend.  I've had a worsening problem with hard starting, but it seems to be less of a problem when it's cold, so I figured replacing wires and plugs should help.  I also haven't done this since I bought the car over 2 years ago.  I picked up Bosch Platinum's for the plugs, and Niehoff wires.<P>What I'm wondering is if there will be any forseeable difficulties when doing this?  Keep in mind I've never changed plugs and wires before.  I plan on taking off the decklid to get at the front ones, and I've already been forewarned to only remove one wire at a time.  (The friend who warned me of this didn't...  the first time  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">).  So if anyone would describe what to do from there, it'd help.  Especially what exactly I use the silicon stuff that came with the wires on.  Thanks!<P>Brandon<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Field_Front_(sig)_1.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||G-Nasty||11-19-2001||12:36 AM||||Crawl into trunk and reach into frontside of engine. Rest ass on trunk seal-If your hands and arms are too fat to fit within working area then I advise getting a skinny friend to replace plugs.<BR>Both Chiltons and Hyanes manuals say to do this... Also, ceramic part of plug may break but dont worry-they still come out. Use Craftsman tools ONLY! Cheap chinese tools will only aggrevate the process and your fiero. Changing plugs in these cars is a nightmare so take your time and remain calm-Prepare to use profanity. Good luck bro- ||||reg||1
Z||000002||RossT||11-19-2001||12:37 AM||||I just did the plugs on my formula.  No need to take off the decklid. Use a small mirror and stand in the trunk.  Not really that hard at all, and  it was my first time.  Make sure you use a littl anti-seize on the new plugs.||||reg||1
Z||000003||My7Fieros||11-19-2001||12:41 AM||||You pretty much already said it. Go ahead and take off the decklid...some people will say you dont need to, but believe me, its super easy to do and helps alot.<BR>Make sure you gap the plugs...get a plug gapper at pretty much anywhere auto supplies are sold. Make sure you have a spark plug socket...cant remember the size, but youll know it..its got a rubber ring inside.<BR>You may want to make a cheap investment and buy a swivel extention for your wratchet as well....once again, makes things MUCH easier. And you already said only doing one wire at a time....DEFINANTLY. <BR>Other than that..theres nothing to it.<BR>1. Remove decklid<BR>2. Gap plugs<BR>3. Take out <B>1</B> plug, replace, put wire back on....do this until all the plugs are done.<BR>4. Take off <B>1</B> wire at a time and replace w/new wire (youll have to put them side by side for the one that matches in length).<BR>5. Replace decklid...<BR>Voila! Not a big deal at all....||||reg||7
Z||000004||camon||11-19-2001||02:33 AM||||About a month ago was my first time replacing plugs and the job wasn't that hard.<P>I removed both engine vents (left the engine compartment hood on) stood in the trunk used one hand to feel around the backside of the engine (to locate plugs) then used that hand to guide the socket to the plug (I used a short extension on the socket and ratchet). I had two of the ceramic insulators brake off but the plugs still came out pretty easily. Just remember.....<BR>Replace them one at a time and be ready to get your hands and arms real dirty.<P>Hope it goes as well for you as it did for me (took about 5 minutes total for the rear plugs)!<P>------------------<BR>Keith||||reg||12
Z||000005||tgowens||11-19-2001||02:55 AM||||Brandon86SE, I replace plugs and wires all the time and it is not hard to do - I use a stubby 3/8 ratchet with a short extension and a swivel spark plug socket and I never take the deck lid off to do it. The primary thing to remember when replacing the wires is this...... the important one is the #1 plug and since it is on the trunk side on the passenger side of the car just leave that one for last -- the connector on the distributor cap that the #1 wire is connected to is obviously #1 - then just go around the distributor in a clockwise direction with #2 (from the front passenger side), #3 middle trunk side, #4 middle front, #5 rear driver's side, and finally #6 front driver's side.....<P>Piece of cake! (and you don't have to scribe and remove the deck lid to do it! - that saves time.......)<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_TGO.jpg"> <P><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Pyric||11-19-2001||10:01 AM||||Be careful when in the trunk. If you sit back you can crack the tail-light lenses. Also if those are Bosche 4+ plugs monitor how the care runs after replacing them.  I've heard they can cause the engine to miss. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||tesmith66||11-19-2001||12:29 PM||||All I do is run a ratcheting tie-down strap from the trunk latch to the front (firewall side) engine lifting loop. Unbolt the dogbone on one end, swing it out of the way and ratchet the engine back enough to get at the front plugs.<P>You can also get to them from below, but that sucks.<P>As with anything back there, take your time and be careful.<P>Also, put a dab of dialectric grease in both ends of the wires. This ensures a good connection, keeps corrosion away, and keeps the boot from sticking like a mofo to the plug.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Good luck!<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||4
Z||000008||Brandon86SE||12-06-2001||03:50 PM||||Alright, I know I'm bad, and shoulda asked earlier, but me and my friends have been trying to figure this out, but to no avail.  We got all the wires changed, and the trunkside plugs, but the ones in the back are a mystery.<P>We get the ratchet on them, and the try to turn, and it turns rather easy compared to how tight the ones in the front were.  The ratchet is clicking as we work, but this continues on for a long time, and the plug is not even loosened.  There was some built up grease and such back there, but I can't imagine that it's enough to get in the way of the ratchet getting all the way on the plug.....<P>So ya, me and my friends are stumped...... any suggestions?<P>One thing we were thinking is that someone put in a different size plug in the back, but my friend didn't have his other socket there yesterday when we were trying to see if that would do it.<P>Thanks,<P>Brandon||||reg||5
Z||000009||tgowens||12-06-2001||06:09 PM||||It sounds to me like the spark plug socket isn't seated on the plug. Usually the socket has a rubber ring in it which will hold the plug once it is removed from the block. You should be able to "feel" when the socket is seated on the plug properly.||||reg||1
Z||000010||VISCERAL||12-06-2001||06:11 PM||||Lose the Bosch Platinums. I have tried them on four of my GM american cars w/v6's and v8's. They will run well for a week then run like crap. I had all new wires, cap and rotor, plugs and it occasionally started to stumble. It went away after I swapped in any other plug. Same effect on all my old cars: 89 camaro v8, 96 TA 5.7 6 speed, Ford Mustang GT, etc.... Go out and get Delco rapidfires in platinum or NGK's U groove's. Listen to me now and believe me later.||||reg||1
Z||000011||avengador1||12-06-2001||07:49 PM||||Place a towel over the engine, This will help so you don't get as dirty. The silicone grease is to help the boot slide over the spark plug easier. Do one wire and plug at a time, make sure you gap the plugs and apply anti-seize compound to the threads of the plugs. This will make them easier to remove in the future and they won't rust to the block. Remove the decklid if you can't fit your arms behind the engine it gives you more space to work, but you risk damaging your decklid. Don't use the Bosch plus 4 plugs they only are good for a short while then the car runs crappy. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
